# Exxact's vLLM image for DGX Spark (GB10 / SM121 / aarch64).
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
# ---------------
# The quickstart previously depended on two images it should not ship:
#   * ghcr.io/bjk110/vllm-spark -- a third-party community build. Good work by a
#     real person with a public Apache-2.0 build repo, but not something to ask
#     Exxact customers to pull.
#   * vllm-spark-qwen35-lmhead-fix:local -- a `docker commit` of a live
#     container. No Dockerfile, in no registry, rebuildable by nobody. Anyone
#     cloning this repo simply cannot run the Qwen profile.
#
# The base below is NVIDIA's own NGC vLLM: public guest-pull, multi-arch, and
# published with a cosign signature, SBOM and VEX. Pinned by digest, never by
# tag, so a silent upstream re-push cannot change what customers run.
#
# WHAT THE ONE PATCH FIXES
# ------------------------
# NGC 26.07-py3 ships vLLM 0.24.0+092c4842.dev, whose tool-parser code does:
#
#     from xgrammar import StructuralTag, normalize_tool_choice
#         -- vllm/tool_parsers/structural_tag_registry.py:12
#
# but its metadata pins `xgrammar==0.2.0`, and 0.2.0 does not export
# normalize_tool_choice. The image is internally inconsistent: it starts,
# serves, and answers chat perfectly, while EVERY tool call fails with
# "cannot import name 'normalize_tool_choice' from 'xgrammar'".
#
# That is the dangerous shape of bug -- an agent whose tools never fire looks
# like a stupid model, not a broken install. Verified on a Spark 2026-08-21;
# 0.2.1 is the earliest release exporting the symbol.
#
# Note what is NOT patched here: the qwen3_5 lm_head quant_config fix that the
# local image was built for is already present upstream (vLLM PR #42124, first
# released in 0.23.0) and in this base. Do not re-add it.
FROM nvcr.io/nvidia/vllm@sha256:95c498a475142c20c989c65e5d223348c09fed83ba17ddf44f117610c0bd3268

RUN pip install --no-cache-dir "xgrammar==0.2.1"

# Fail the BUILD rather than a customer's install if the symbol is still absent.
RUN python3 -c "import xgrammar; assert hasattr(xgrammar, 'normalize_tool_choice'), 'xgrammar is missing normalize_tool_choice'" \
 && python3 -c "import vllm; print('vllm', vllm.__version__)"
